3-11-20T13:05:15.000Z",null,null,""],["github:talkingrobots/geocompros",0,"talkingrobots/GeoCompROS","https://github.com/talkingrobots/GeoCompROS","ROS Library for performing fast geometrical computations. Was developed for functional mapping in NIFTI.",0,31,"2013-11-29T09:45:50.000Z",11,13,31,0,0,[],8,0,"talkingrobots/GeoCompROS",0,1,0,4,4,0,"2013-11-29T09:58:54.000Z",null,null,""],["github:talkingrobots/nifti_infieldpicsserver",0,"talkingrobots/NIFTi_InFieldPicsServer","https://github.com/talkingrobots/NIFTi_InFieldPicsServer","The In-Field Pictures Server is a central hub to exchange pictures between different actors connected to a ROS network. First, the server reads camera images from a ROS topic, analyzes them, and saves them locally. Additionally, anyone can move pictures into this folder and the server will also analyze them. EXIF information is extracted and meta-data is stored. All of the analyzed pictures are broadcast within a few seconds with attached meta-data. Anyone listening to that ROS topic receives the pictures and their later modifications, if any.  In NIFTi, we used this software to share pictures of the explored environments between various actors of a search and rescue team, such as mission commanders, robot pilots, and in-field rescuers. The Operator Control Units (OCUs) that the pilots used had a button to take snapshots of camera feeds that were sent to the server, analyzed, and broadcast as a picture localized picture within the ROS map. All pictures could be accessed from these OCUs. Additionally, synchronized the server's folder with a Dropbox account for the team. In-field rescuers also synchronized their cameras with the same Dropbox account so that their pictures would be tr",0,31,"2013-11-29T10:29:01.000Z",5,13,32,0,0,[],8,0,"talkingrobots/NIFTi_InFieldPicsServer",1,2,0,4,4,0,"2013-11-29T10:46:19.000Z",null,null,""],["github:talkingrobots/nifti_ocu",0,"talkingrobots/NIFTi_OCU","https://github.com/talkingrobots/NIFTi_OCU","The Operator Control Unit (OCU) is a multi-modal control software for the NIFTi robots. The OCU integrates input from all partners of the NIFTi project as it was designed specifically for that project. However, it can be used for any standard ROS-enabled robot. The basic inputs/outputs are twist messages, maps, point clouds, camera feeds, etc.  If need be, the OCU can be modified to read from different topics or display different elements. The code is heavily based on RVIZ, therefore RVIZ can be used as a debugging tool for extending the OCU.  One of the obvious advantages over RVIZ is the possibility to display up to four view points on the scene at once. For example, a top-down view of the environment, a close-up 3D view following the robot, a panoramic view, and a thermal camera output overlaid with the virtual scene. Scientific paper: http://ieeexplore.ieee.org/xpl/login.jsp?tp=&arnumber=6343896&url=http%3A%2F%2Fieeexplore.ieee.org%2Fxpls%2Fabs_all.jsp%3Farnumber%3D6343896",0,31,"2013-11-29T09:36:57.000Z",5,13,33,0,0,[],8,0,"talkingrobots/NIFTi_OCU",2,0,0,4,4,0,"2013-11-29T11:44:58.000Z",null,null,""],["github:talkingrobots/nifti_tftopathconverter",0,"talkingrobots/NIFTi_TFToPathConverter","https://github.com/talkingrobots/NIFTi_TFToPathConverter","The TF to Path Converter listens to the ROS TFs to accumulate the robot's position over time and publish it as a path.",0,31,"2013-11-29T10:19:19.000Z",11,13,34,0,0,[],8,0,"talkingrobots/NIFTi_TFToPathConverter",0,0,0,4,4,0,"2013-11-29T10:21:51.000Z",null,null,""],["github:tcia/national-biomedical-image-archive",0,"TCIA/national-biomedical-image-archive","https://github.com/TCIA/national-biomedical-image-archive","The National Biomedical Imaging Archive (NBIA) is a free and open source service and software application that enables users to securely store, search, and download diagnostic medical images, providing a searchable national repository integrating in vivo cancer images with clinical and genomic data. Using role-based security, NBIA provides web-based access to de-identified DICOM images, image markup, annotations, and rich meta data.",0,32,"2013-11-03T03:11:53.000Z",1,1,35,0,0,[],0,1,"TCIA/national-biomedical-image-archive",1,0,0,0,6,0,"2016-03-18T21:52:07.000Z",null,null,""],["github:termiko/dicoogle",0,"termiko/DICoogle","https://github.com/termiko/DICoogle","mirror of Dicoogle - DICOM repository indexer",0,33,"2013-11-06T08:28:18.000Z",9,10,36,0,0,[],0,0,"termiko/DICoogle",0,2,0,0,6,0,"2013-06-17T12:54:16.000Z",null,null,""],["github:vivabrain/angiotk",0,"vivabrain/angiotk","https://github.com/vivabrain/angiotk","Virtual Angiography Toolkit",0,34,"2013-11-08T11:06:49.000Z",5,14,37,0,0,[],9,1,"vivabrain/angiotk",4,3,0,3,4,0,"2018-03-31T06:27:22.000Z",null,null,""],["github:wittend/jsdicom-lib",0,"wittend/jsdicom-lib","https://github.com/wittend/jsdicom-lib","dicom library in javascript",0,35,"2013-11-13T18:15:25.000Z",1,1,38,0,0,[],0,0,"wittend/jsdicom-lib",0,0,0,0,9,0,"2015-04-01T20:57:41.000Z",null,null,""],["github:wittend/node-dicom",0,"wittend/node-dicom","https://github.com/wittend/node-dicom","DICOM utils/parser for node.js",0,35,"2013-11-13T18:30:31.000Z",1,1,39,0,0,[],0,0,"wittend/node-dicom",0,0,0,2,12,0,"2015-04-01T21:03:48.000Z",null,null,""],["github:wittend/webdicom",0,"wittend/webDICOM","https://github.com/wittend/webDICOM","Web based DICOM viewer",0,35,"2013-11-13T18:15:53.000Z",1,1,40,0,0,[],0,0,"wittend/webDICOM",0,0,0,0,13,0,"2014-01-23T16:38:35.000Z",null,null,""],["github:yjsyyyjszf/clearcanvasmip",0,"yjsyyyjszf/ClearCanvasMip","https://github.com/yjsyyyjszf/ClearCanvasMip","ClearCanvas using simpleITK",0,36,"2013-11-23T08:21:58.000Z",6,15,41,0,0,[],8,0,"yjsyyyjszf/ClearCanvasMip",0,0,0,0,1,0,"2013-11-23T08:21:58.000Z",null,null,""]]}
